        "problem": "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation ${x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+1=0$ for $x>0?$",
        "worked_solution": "Rearranging and Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =-\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-1,0,1$ for $x>0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011667/assets/c837c3b617fa49f3d081d961196a954fada99b8d7cd12b7d7f729232e16be066.png)",
